WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.905
Send a message across a network, and
many computers see it on the way.

2
00:00:09.125 --> 00:00:15.487
A cipher turns cat into 3 1 20. Only
the key turns it back into words.

3
00:00:15.987 --> 00:00:20.167
A message on a network passes
through many computers on its way.

4
00:00:20.387 --> 00:00:25.123
Every one of them can see it. Without
a cipher, they can read it too.

5
00:00:25.343 --> 00:00:30.220
A cipher changes the message by a
rule. Now it looks like nonsense.

6
00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:34.666
Only the person with the rule can
read it. That rule is the key.

7
00:00:34.886 --> 00:00:39.762
A cipher does not hide your message.
They can see it but not read it.

8
00:00:39.982 --> 00:00:44.254
Passwords, bank details and private
messages all travel this way.

9
00:00:44.754 --> 00:00:48.609
The alphabet, a to z, is
twenty-six letters long.

10
00:00:48.829 --> 00:00:53.008
Give every letter its number.
a is 1, b is 2, c is 3.

11
00:00:53.228 --> 00:00:56.201
Keep counting all the
way to z, which is 26.

12
00:00:56.421 --> 00:01:02.086
Careful: j is 10, not 9. Count along
the strip and you will not slip.

13
00:01:02.306 --> 00:01:06.068
That rule, 1 is a and 2 is
b, is the key to this cipher.

14
00:01:06.568 --> 00:01:09.726
To write a word in code,
take one letter at a time.

15
00:01:09.946 --> 00:01:14.358
cat starts with c. Find
c on the strip: c is 3.

16
00:01:14.578 --> 00:01:19.454
Then a is 1, and t is 20.
So cat in code is 3, 1, 20.

17
00:01:19.674 --> 00:01:22.321
The same letter is always the same number.

18
00:01:22.541 --> 00:01:26.210
So bee is 2, 5, 5. The e is 5 both times.

19
00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:29.960
Decoding goes the other way.
You start with the numbers.

20
00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:34.499
To decode 8 9, find the
eighth letter. It is h.

21
00:01:34.719 --> 00:01:37.877
The ninth letter is i. So 8 9 says hi.

22
00:01:38.097 --> 00:01:42.741
A longer one now: 19 5
3 18 5 20 says secret.

23
00:01:42.961 --> 00:01:45.887
Without the key, 8 9 is just two numbers.

24
00:01:46.387 --> 00:01:50.241
A message has two forms, and
each one has its own name.

25
00:01:50.461 --> 00:01:53.852
Before you do anything to it,
the message is plain text.

26
00:01:54.072 --> 00:01:57.972
Now apply the rule. What comes
out is called ciphertext.

27
00:01:58.192 --> 00:02:03.579
hat is the plain text. 8 1 20 is
the same message as ciphertext.

28
00:02:03.799 --> 00:02:07.654
Apply the rule the other way,
and you get the plain text back.

29
00:02:08.154 --> 00:02:12.426
The same letter is always the same
number. That makes it easy to use.

30
00:02:12.646 --> 00:02:17.941
It is also its weakness. Count the
numbers in a long coded message.

31
00:02:18.161 --> 00:02:23.548
The one that turns up most is probably
e. In English, e is the commonest letter.

32
00:02:23.768 --> 00:02:28.644
Somebody can start there and work out
the rest. They never needed the key.

33
00:02:29.144 --> 00:02:33.695
A cipher changes a message by
a rule. That rule is the key.

34
00:02:33.915 --> 00:02:39.163
Encode cat and you get 3 1
20. Decode 8 9 and you get hi.

35
00:02:39.383 --> 00:02:45.513
Plain text goes in, ciphertext comes
out, and without the key it is nonsense.
